{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788225917,"nonce":"mlI5bcgaTLLs+f6O+Q3BqI52AHE=","message":"SEQUENCING — Evan, session 11, 2026-08-31: **3.3 (`1adc737`) goes first, as already specced; this issue is the follow-on.**\n\nHis reasoning: 3.3 is the fleshed-out one — it has a full scope, a shipped template corpus and a concrete acceptance rehearsal, so it should be proven before a composition layer is built on top of it. Prove `place-layout` can put ONE room down correctly at all; then make rooms tile.\n\nThat order also front-loads the risk sensibly. 3.3's rehearsal is a single free-standing 5x7 bedroom, and a one-cell placement slide there is cosmetic — so 3.3 can be proven even while `acee526` (1.9) is still open. This issue cannot: on a grid a slide is CUMULATIVE and silently de-phases every module after it. So 1.9 blocks the grid but not the first room, and doing 3.3 first means 1.9 does not gate the whole line of work.\n\nNothing above is withdrawn — the module size, the shared-wall semantics and the off-phase refusal all still stand as the decision. They are just next, not now.","files":null}]}